        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-what-ran">
            <title>Part 3: What Just Ran</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the top-down walk paragraph(s) from
                 the passed slides — the two #defines, the loop, and
                 lsm303_AccelRegisterRead() as one call into the Day 10
                 library (the five operations of subsec-day10-library,
                 i2c1_memRead() among them).  The (0x32 >> 1) planting
                 sentence and the what-0x33-proves sentence below are
                 load-bearing and written now. -->

            <p>
                Here is the whole of <c>whoami_test.c</c>.  Notice the first
                <c>#define</c>: the device address is written
                <c>(0x32 &gt;&gt; 1)</c> — a shift, not a plain number.  We'll
                see why once the logic analyzer is on the bus.
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include "i2c.h"

#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL       (0x32 >> 1)  // 7-bit address 0b0011001
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A		   0x0F	  		// default value: 00110011

uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

int main(void) {
	uint8_t who_am_i = 0;

	i2c1_init();
	uart2_init();
	printf("LSM303AGR initialization test \n\r");

	while(1) {
		who_am_i = lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A);
		if( who_am_i == 0x33 ) {
			printf("Accelerometer initialized! \n\r");
		}
		else {
			printf("Could not connect to accelerometer \n\r");
		}
		delay_ms(1000);
	}
	return 0;
}

/* Read one byte from an accelerometer register (Datasheet, Table 22) */
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

 	return data;
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The one function this program defines,
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c>, is a single call into the
                I2C library from <xref ref="ch-i2c"/> —
                <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>, asked for one byte from one register.
                And a successful 0x33 proves what the reading said it proves:
                the wiring, the address, and the read path all work.  It is
                not yet evidence about how the sensor is configured — that
                work is still ahead of us.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-whoami-main">
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL       (0x32 >> 1)  // 7-bit address 0b0011001
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A		   0x0F	  		// default value: 00110011

int main(void) {
	uint8_t who_am_i = 0;
	i2c1_init();
	uart2_init();
	printf("LSM303AGR initialization test \n\r");

	while(1) {
		who_am_i = lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A);
		if( who_am_i == 0x33 ) {
			printf("Accelerometer initialized! \n\r");
		}
		else {
			printf("Could not connect to accelerometer \n\r");
		}
		delay_ms(1000);
	}
	return 0;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Walk it top down: the loop reads WHO_AM_I once a second, forever. Say out loud that the address is written (0x32 &gt;&gt; 1) — and that we are not explaining the shift yet; the analyzer will. The listing is abridged for the wall (#includes, the prototype and two blank lines are off it); the full file is in the book and in Src.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-whoami-read">
    <p><c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c> is a single call into the I2C library we have been using since the display — <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>, asked for one byte from one register:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Read one byte from an accelerometer register (Datasheet, Table 22) */
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

 	return data;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Recall the five library operations from Day 10 by name — init, byteWrite, byteRead, memWrite, memRead — this is the last of them, asked for one byte.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-whoami-proves">
    <ul>
        <li>A successful 0x33 proves the whole read path: the wiring, the device address, the protocol, and the code all had to work for that value to arrive.</li>
        <li>It is not yet evidence about how the sensor is configured — the control registers are still at their power-on defaults, and setting them up is work still ahead of us.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. This is the reading's WHO_AM_I point, said once in class: proof of communication, not of configuration.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-analyzer">
            <title>Part 4: Put the Analyzer on It</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the lead-in paragraph naming the
                 Day 9x setup as a recall (one sentence, before the wiring
                 paragraph below).
                 P-6: act-day13-predict-decoder and act-day13-capture come
                 BEFORE fig-whoami-capture, which answers them.  Do not
                 reorder. -->

            <p>
                The analyzer setup is the one from Day 9x, unchanged
                (<xref ref="fig-waveforms-setup"/> and
                <xref ref="fig-waveforms-decode"/>): the AD2's digital
                channels — the larger flying-lead bundle — go DIO0 (pink) to
                the SDA row, DIO1 (green) to the SCL row, and black to ground.
                In Waveforms, trigger on DIO0 falling, take a single sweep,
                and add the I2C decoder with DIO1 as clock and DIO0 as data.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-analyzer-wiring" ref="fig-waveforms-setup" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>This is the setup from Day 9x, unchanged: the AD2's digital channels — the larger flying-lead bundle — go DIO0 (pink) to the SDA row, DIO1 (green) to the SCL row, and black to ground.</li>
        <li>In Waveforms: trigger on DIO0 falling, take a single sweep, and add the I2C decoder with DIO1 as clock and DIO0 as data.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 5 min. The wiring is the recall; do not re-teach the decoder steps — the screenshot has them, and the room did this on Day 9x.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-predict-decoder">
                <title>Predict what the decoder will show</title>
                <statement>
                    <p><c>whoami_test.c</c> is running: once a second, the
                    loop calls <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c>, which is one
                    call to <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>.  Before you capture
                    anything, predict what the protocol decoder will show for
                    one pass through the loop.  How many transactions?  What
                    goes out in each one, and what comes back?  Write your
                    prediction down.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-predict-decoder">
                <p>Two transactions, one right after the other.  The decoder
                reads: <c>h19 WR</c>, ACK, <c>h0F</c>, ACK, STOP — the device
                address and the register address going out — then
                <c>h19 RD</c>, ACK, <c>h33</c>, NACK, STOP — the device
                address again, and the register's contents coming back.  Most
                predictions get the pieces right and the <em>two
                transactions</em> wrong: our <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> ends the
                first transaction and starts a second one.  That, the 0x19,
                and the final NACK are what Part 5 digs into — let the room
                find them on their own traces first.</p>
            </instructor>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-predict-code" room="yes">
    <p>Predict, in writing, before you capture: when this loop runs, what will the protocol decoder show?  How many transactions, and what bytes in each one?</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL  (0x32 >> 1)   // 7-bit address 0b0011001
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A     0x0F          // default value: 00110011

// called from the loop in main(), once a second:
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

 	return data;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 4 min, committed in writing. The listing is abridged to the lines the prediction needs; the full file was just walked and is in the book. Collect two or three predictions out loud without ruling on them — most get the pieces right and the number of transactions wrong, and the trace settles it, not you.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-capture">
                <title>Capture the WHO_AM_I read</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-analyzer-wiring">
                    <statement><p>Wire the AD2's digital leads: DIO0 (pink) to
                    the SDA row, DIO1 (green) to the SCL row, black to ground.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-sweep">
                    <statement><p>In Waveforms' logic mode, set the trigger to
                    DIO0 falling and take a single sweep.  Add the I2C decoder
                    with DIO1 as clock and DIO0 as data.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-read-decode">
                    <statement><p>Read your decoded trace and find three
                    things: the device address the decoder prints, the
                    register address, and the value the accelerometer sent
                    back.  Compare them with the two <c>#define</c>s in
                    <c>whoami_test.c</c> and with your prediction.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture">
                <caption>The WHO_AM_I read, captured and decoded.  Two
                transactions cross the screen: <c>h19 WR</c>, its ACK, the
                register address <c>h0F</c>, its ACK, and a STOP — then
                <c>h19 RD</c>, its ACK, the returned <c>h33</c>, a NACK, and
                the final STOP.  SCL is DIO1 (green) and SDA is DIO0 (pink),
                as wired on Day 9x.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_c.png" width="97%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-c" ref="fig-whoami-capture">
    <caption>Two transactions: h19 WR · A · h0F · A · P, then h19 RD · A · h33 · N · P.  Find all three values on your own screen.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min, and the Part's rescue: a student who cannot get a sweep marks this projected capture instead — the decoded values are the same by construction. Checkpoint at minute 65 is the end of this Part.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-digging-deeper">
            <title>Part 5: Digging Deeper</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the lead-in sentence (the questions
                 are asked of the room's own traces), and the recap sentence
                 placing the remaining Digging Deeper questions (who sends the
                 address, where the register address is said, where the data
                 appears) onto fig-i2c-transfer-pattern.  The two-kinds-of-NACK
                 paragraph and the memRead-walk paragraph below are
                 load-bearing and written now.
                 The memRead code walk's LISTING is a <slide> block (delivery
                 2 authors it beside this subsection); the code is quoted
                 verbatim from assets/starters/i2c.c and registered in
                 check_starters.py (FRAGMENTS). -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-digging-deeper">
                <title>Digging deeper — against your own trace</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Answer from the capture on your own screen, not from
                    the book.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-why-19">
                    <statement><p>The header says the device address is 0x32.
                    Why does the decoder print <c>h19</c>?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-address-twice">
                    <statement><p>On Day 10 we told you that our
                    <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> sends the register address, lets
                    that transaction end, and starts a second one.  Confirm it
                    on your trace: find where the first transaction ends and
                    the second begins, and what the device address is doing
                    there a second time.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-who-acks">
                    <statement><p>Three acknowledge bits cross your screen:
                    after the first <c>h19</c>, after <c>h0F</c>, and after
                    <c>h33</c>.  For each one — who sent it, and is it an ACK
                    or a NACK?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-digging-deeper">
                <p>0x32 &gt;&gt; 1 = 0b00110010 &gt;&gt; 1 = 0b0011001 = 0x19.
                The datasheet gives the 8-bit form with the R/W bit attached;
                the decoder prints the 7-bit address, which is what actually
                identifies the device — Day 9x's shift, paying out.</p>
                <p>The address goes out twice because the read is two
                transactions: a write that delivers the register address, a
                STOP, then a fresh START and the same address with the read
                bit.  The datasheet's diagram joins the halves with a repeated
                START instead; ours lets the bus go idle in between, which
                works with one controller on the bus.</p>
                <p>The ACKs after <c>h19</c> and <c>h0F</c> are the
                accelerometer's.  The bit after <c>h33</c> is a NACK, and it
                is the <em>controller's</em>, sent on purpose: one byte was
                asked for, one byte arrived, and the NACK says <q>no
                more</q>.</p>
            </instructor>

            <p>
                That last answer is worth a name, because two different things
                on this bus are both called NACK.  The NACK on your trace is
                the <em>controller's</em>, and it is part of a successful
                read: the controller asked for one byte, got it, and
                deliberately does not acknowledge, which tells the target to
                stop sending.  The other kind of NACK comes from nobody at
                all: if no device recognizes the address, nothing pulls SDA
                LOW during the acknowledge bit, and the wire simply stays
                HIGH.  Same name, opposite meanings — one ends a read on
                purpose, the other means nobody answered.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-two-nacks">
    <ul>
        <li>The NACK on your trace is the <em>controller's</em>, and it is part of a successful read: one byte was asked for, one byte arrived, and by not acknowledging, the controller ends the read.</li>
        <li>The other kind of NACK comes from nobody at all: if no device recognizes the address, nothing pulls SDA LOW during the acknowledge bit, and the wire simply stays HIGH.</li>
        <li>Same name, opposite meanings — one ends a read on purpose, the other means nobody answered.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Name both kinds now — the second one is about to be produced on purpose.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-transfer-pattern" ref="fig-i2c-transfer-pattern">
    <caption>The datasheet's own drawing of what you captured.  One difference to find: where it draws SR — a repeated START — your trace shows a STOP and a fresh START.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Answer the remaining Digging Deeper questions on this diagram: the controller sends the address; the register address goes out as SUB in the write half; the data comes back from the target in the read half. The master/slave wording is the datasheet's own — we say controller and target.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The transfer diagram in <xref ref="fig-i2c-transfer-pattern"/>
                is the datasheet's own drawing of what you captured — the
                write pattern and the read pattern, with every field named.
                Where our trace shows a STOP and a fresh START between the two
                halves of the read, the datasheet draws a repeated START; the
                register-level walk of <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> in
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-library"/> shows exactly where our
                library makes that choice, and why it matters on a bus with
                two controllers.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-memread-write">
    <p>Everything you captured came from one function, and it makes your trace's <em>first</em> transaction like this:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void i2c1_memRead(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t registerAddr, uint8_t nbytes, uint8_t *data) {
	// Ping target address for writing 1 byte (the register address)
	I2C1->CR2 = (deviceAddr<<1)                  // target address
			    | (1U << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos)     // for registerAddr
				| I2C_CR2_AUTOEND                // Send Stop condition after transfer
			    | I2C_CR2_START;				 // Send Start condition
	while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );		 // Wait until Start is received

	if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {		  // Make sure ACK received
		// Send register address
		while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_TXIS) );     // Wait for Tx to be ready
		I2C1->TXDR = registerAddr;			      // Send register address
	}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 3 min, the first in-class walk of this code (the fact it explains — stop-then-start — is Day 10's). One write to CR2 describes the whole transaction before any of it happens: who, how many bytes, AUTOEND, START — Day 10's byteWrite pattern. AUTOEND here is where our library chooses a STOP where the datasheet draws a repeated START. Trailing comments are shortened for the wall; the full commentary is in i2c.c and the ch-i2c Reference.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-memread-read">
    <p>Then the same function starts over — a second CR2 write, this time with the read bit — and that is your trace's <em>second</em> transaction:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Restart, with read request for n bytes
	I2C1->CR2 = (deviceAddr<<1)                  // target address
			    | (nbytes << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos) // will read n bytes
				| I2C_CR2_RD_WRN                 // ping for reading
			    | I2C_CR2_AUTOEND                // Send Stop condition after transfer
				| I2C_CR2_START;				 // Send Start condition
	while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );		 // Wait until Start is received

	// Read the data
	if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {			// Make sure ACK received
		for(uint8_t i=0; i<nbytes; i++) {
			while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_RXNE) );		// Wait for Rx to be ready
			data[i] = I2C1->RXDR;						// Read the data
		}
	}
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 3 min. RD_WRN is the only new field: same address, n bytes, AUTOEND, START. Between the two CR2 writes the bus went idle — STOP, then a fresh START — which is what put the device address on your screen twice. On a bus with two controllers the register pointer could be changed between our two transactions; the register-level detail is in the ch-i2c Reference. Match each piece to the trace on their screens as you go.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-break-it">
            <title>Part 6: Break It on Purpose</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the debrief paragraph on the
                 asymmetry the room saw, WRITTEN ONLY AFTER Q3 RESOLVES —
                 no student-facing sentence may assert what the program
                 PRINTS on a NACK until Petra confirms the library's
                 behavior (ground truth §2a, §9 Q3).  The trace side (one
                 transaction, NACK after the address) is verified and may be
                 asserted.  The "expected here" line is SPOKEN (presenter
                 note on the Part 6 commit slide), never printed. -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-break-it">
                <title>Break it: a wrong device address</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>A NACK is easier to recognize the first time if you
                    produce one on purpose, on a bus you know is healthy.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-break-predict">
                    <statement><p>Change the <c>0x32</c> in
                    <c>LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL</c> to <c>0x30</c> and rebuild —
                    but before you run it, predict: what will CoolTerm show,
                    and what will the logic analyzer show?  Write both
                    predictions down.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-break-capture">
                    <statement><p>Arm a single sweep on the analyzer (trigger
                    on DIO0 falling), then press the board's reset button to
                    catch the first transaction after startup.  Read the
                    decoded row: what happened after the address byte?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-break-restore">
                    <statement><p>Change the address back to <c>0x32</c>,
                    rebuild, and confirm the link works again before
                    Part 7.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-break-it">
                <p>On the wire: one transaction.  <c>h18 WR</c> goes out
                (0x30 &gt;&gt; 1), no device recognizes it, SDA stays HIGH
                through the acknowledge bit — the decoder prints
                <c>N</c> — and a STOP ends it.  This is the second kind of
                NACK from Part 5: the one from nobody at all.  Single sweep
                then reset is the reliable way to catch it.  Do not assert
                what CoolTerm prints in this case — collect the room's
                observations instead.  (Petra is checking the library's
                wrong-address behavior; the affected sentence lands after
                that.)</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture-nack">
                <caption>The same read attempted at the wrong device address.
                One transaction: <c>h18 WR</c> goes out, no device answers,
                the acknowledge bit stays HIGH (the decoder prints
                <c>N</c>), and a STOP ends the transaction.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_a.png" width="97%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-a" ref="fig-whoami-capture-nack">
    <caption>One transaction: h18 WR goes out, nothing pulls SDA LOW at the acknowledge bit — the decoder prints N — and a STOP ends it.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min, the projected rescue for this capture too. This is the second kind of NACK: the one from nobody at all. Collect what the room's CoolTerm windows showed rather than asserting it — the library's wrong-address behavior is still being checked (Q3), and nothing student-facing may claim what the program prints here.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-break-stretch">
                <title>If you finish early: a wrong register address</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-stretch-register">
                    <statement><p>Restore the device address, and this time
                    read a wrong <em>register</em>: call
                    <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(0x20)</c> instead of the
                    WHO_AM_I address.  Predict first — will the transactions
                    complete this time?  What value comes back?  Then capture
                    it, and check the value you got against the LSM303AGR
                    datasheet, §8.6 (CTRL_REG1_A).</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-stretch-display">
                    <statement><p>One more prediction, no capture needed yet:
                    the display on your bus answers to address 0x70.  If you
                    asked <em>it</em> for register 0x0F the way this program
                    asks the accelerometer, what would happen at the
                    acknowledge bit after the address byte — and can the value
                    that comes back be 0x33's kind of proof?  You have
                    everything on your desk to check.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-break-stretch">
                <p>With the right device address and a wrong register
                address, everything acknowledges: the accelerometer is being
                addressed correctly, so both transactions complete, and it
                returns whatever register 0x20 holds.  At power-on that is
                0x07 — CTRL_REG1_A's default, 0b00000111, straight from the
                datasheet's §8.6 — so the wire shows a perfectly healthy read
                of a value that is simply not 0x33; the captured trace below
                shows exactly this.</p>
                <p>For the display question: the address 0x70 gets an ACK —
                the display is on the bus and recognizes it — so the
                transactions complete.  Whatever byte comes back, it is not a
                WHO_AM_I value, because the HT16K33 has no such register;
                that is the point.  A completed read proves you reached
                <em>a</em> device — the WHO_AM_I value is what proves it is
                the right one.  The actual byte returned is discoverable on
                their own bus; let anyone who captures it report it rather
                than asserting it.</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture-wrong-register">
                <caption>The right device address, the wrong register
                address: reading register 0x20 instead of WHO_AM_I.  Both
                transactions complete — every acknowledge is an ACK — and the
                value that comes back is <c>h07</c>: CTRL_REG1_A's power-on
                default, 0b00000111 (Datasheet §8.6).  A healthy bus,
                reading the wrong thing perfectly.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_b.png" width="97%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-b" ref="fig-whoami-capture-wrong-register">
    <caption>Right device, wrong register: every acknowledge is an ACK, and the value that comes back is h07 — CTRL_REG1_A's power-on default.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The stretch's reveal — or the instructor demo of capture B if the stretch tier was cut for time. A healthy bus, reading the wrong thing perfectly.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 7 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-driver-begins">
            <title>Part 7: A Driver Begins</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose.  OWES: the layers paragraph re-showing
                 fig-firmware-layers (Day 10's figure — xref only, refPage on
                 the slide, NEVER a second xml:id); the second-verse framing
                 sentence (the display was the first device driver, this is
                 the second); and the closing sentence pointing at the four
                 prototypes (one running, one tonight's homework).  The
                 recipe list, the header excerpt and the datasheet moment are
                 load-bearing and written now. -->

            <p>
                Writing a device driver follows the same five steps it
                followed for the display on Day 10
                (<xref ref="fig-firmware-layers"/> is the map — the driver
                talks to the device only through the I2C library, and the
                application talks only to the driver):
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><p>From the datasheet, understand the basic I2C transfers
                for reading and writing.</p></li>
                <li><p>From the datasheet, <c>#define</c> the device's
                addresses and commands.</p></li>
                <li><p>Define functions for the actions you need from the
                device.</p></li>
                <li><p>Write those functions using the <c>i2c.c</c> library
                functions.</p></li>
                <li><p>Devise unit tests to confirm the driver works.</p></li>
            </ol>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-layers" ref="fig-firmware-layers">
    <caption>Day 10's map, back for a second device: the driver reaches the device only through the I2C library, and the application only through the driver.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Re-shown, not re-taught. The display's driver was the first pass through this map; this week you'll build the second — the difference is only which device is at the bottom.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-recipe">
    <p>Writing a device driver follows the same five steps it followed for the display:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>From the datasheet, understand the basic I2C transfers for reading and writing.</li>
        <li>From the datasheet, <c>#define</c> the device's addresses and commands.</li>
        <li>Define functions for the actions you need from the device.</li>
        <li>Write those functions using the <c>i2c.c</c> library functions.</li>
        <li>Devise unit tests to confirm the driver works.</li>
    </ol>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Her slide 28. Steps 1 and 2 are already done for this device — the header is next, and it is step 2 made visible.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Step 2 is already done, and it is worth seeing where it came
                from.  Open the LSM303AGR datasheet to §7, Table 26
                (<em>Register address map</em>, p. 43), and put
                <c>lsm303agr.h</c> beside it: the header <em>is</em> that
                table, transcribed into <c>#define</c>s.  Here is the top of
                its register block:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// I2C address for the sensor
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL    (0x32 >> 1)         // 0011001x

// ACCELEROMETER SECTION
// Accelerometer registers                // DEFAULT    TYPE
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A		   0x0F	  // 00110011	r
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A         0x20   // 00000111   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG2_A         0x21   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG3_A         0x22   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A         0x23   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG5_A         0x24   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG6_A         0x25   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_REFERENCE_A         0x26   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_STATUS_REG_A        0x27   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_OUT_X_L_A           0x28
#define LSM303_OUT_X_H_A           0x29
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_L_A           0x2A
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_H_A           0x2B
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_L_A           0x2C
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_H_A           0x2D
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The real file keeps going — FIFO, interrupt, and click
                registers up to address 0x3D — and we leave all of those at
                their defaults.  Notice the comment columns: for each
                register, the header records the power-on default from the
                datasheet's table.  You met one of those defaults already if
                you did the wrong-register capture.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-header">
    <p>Open the datasheet to §7, Table 26 — the register address map — and put <c>lsm303agr.h</c> beside it: the header is that table, transcribed into <c>#define</c>s.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL    (0x32 >> 1)         // 0011001x

// Accelerometer registers                // DEFAULT    TYPE
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A		   0x0F	  // 00110011	r
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A         0x20   // 00000111   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG2_A         0x21   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG3_A         0x22   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A         0x23   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG5_A         0x24   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG6_A         0x25   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_REFERENCE_A         0x26   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_STATUS_REG_A        0x27   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_OUT_X_L_A           0x28
#define LSM303_OUT_X_H_A           0x29
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_L_A           0x2A
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_H_A           0x2B
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_L_A           0x2C
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_H_A           0x2D
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 2 min. The real file keeps going — FIFO, interrupt and click registers up to 0x3D, all left at their defaults. Point at the DEFAULT column: the wrong-register capture read one of these defaults off the wire.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-header-tour">
                <title>The header is the table</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-header-download">
                    <statement><p>Download <c>lsm303agr.h</c> from Canvas and
                    put it in your <c>mylib</c> folder.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-header-check">
                    <statement><p>In the datasheet's Table 26 (p. 43), find
                    the rows for WHO_AM_I_A and CTRL_REG1_A and check them
                    against the header's <c>#define</c>s — the address and
                    the default value of each.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                Step 3 is also done: the header declares four functions, and
                they are the whole interface the rest of the week builds on.
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// The accelerometer has several gain and update rate settings.  These are described
// in the datasheet and are set up in the AccelInit function.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit();

// The AccelRegisterWrite function should take a register address and a command byte
// to be written into the register.
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data);

// The AccelRegisterRead function should take a register address and return a data byte
// read from the register.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

// Here is the higher-level function to read the acceleration vector.  Output struct
// contains raw (unscaled) acceleration values.
void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s * const result);
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                One of the four is already running on your board —
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c> ran all class.  Writing
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> is tonight's homework.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-prototypes">
    <p>The header also declares the four functions of the driver — the whole interface the rest of the week builds on.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// The accelerometer has several gain and update rate settings.  These are described
// in the datasheet and are set up in the AccelInit function.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit();

// The AccelRegisterWrite function should take a register address and a command byte
// to be written into the register.
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data);

// The AccelRegisterRead function should take a register address and return a data byte
// read from the register.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

// Here is the higher-level function to read the acceleration vector.  Output struct
// contains raw (unscaled) acceleration values.
void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s * const result);
]]></code></program>
    <caption>One of the four is already running — RegisterRead ran all class.  RegisterWrite is tonight's homework, on paper.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Do not open AccelInit or ReadRaw beyond their comments — the settings are the homework's scavenger hunt, and ReadRaw's walk comes with the data format.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 8 -->
